title: "The guided setup assistant"
output: rmarkdown::html_vignette
vignette: >
  %\VignetteIndexEntry{The guided setup assistant}
  %\VignetteEngine{knitr::rmarkdown}
  %\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
---

```{r setup, include = FALSE}
source("precompute-setup.R")
unlink(file.path(docs_root, c("assistant-project", "assistant-sepsis")), recursive = TRUE)
```

`ehr_setup_assistant()` turns a set of choices into a readable R script: project
configuration, download, cohort build, pipeline stages and, optionally, the
analysis and merge calls. Planning is read-only. The assistant never downloads
data or runs a pipeline; you review the script and run the blocks yourself, or
let it write the script to a file.

```{r scid-plan}
sci <- ehr_setup_assistant(
  src_name = "mimic_iv_demo",
  cohort = "scid", import_scope = "hospital",
  base_dir = file.path(docs_root, "assistant-project"),
  interactive = FALSE
)
names(sci)
sci$choices[c("src_name", "cohort", "import_scope", "engine", "db_name")]
```

The generated script starts with the project and the download, then shows each
pipeline stage as its own block:

```{r script-head}
cat(head(sci$script, 24), sep = "\n")
```

A harder case combines the sepsis variant, a multi-source plan, ICU-level
analysis and a merge at the same grain:

```{r sepsis-plan}
sepsis <- ehr_setup_assistant(
  src_name = c("mimic_iv_demo", "eicu_demo"),
  cohort = "sepsis3", sepsis_bundle = "first_sepsis3_admission",
  import_scope = "hospital", pipeline = "full",
  analysis_level = "icu", merge = "icu",
  memory_limit = "4GB", threads = 2,
  base_dir = file.path(docs_root, "assistant-sepsis"),
  interactive = FALSE
)
sepsis$choices[c("src_names", "cohort", "sepsis_bundle", "import_scope",
                 "pipeline", "analysis_level", "merge")]
```

To keep the script, either ask for it at planning time or write the returned
plan later:

```{r save, eval = FALSE}
plan <- ehr_setup_assistant(..., save_script = TRUE,
                            setup_script_path = "setup.R")
write_setup_script(plan, "setup.R", overwrite = TRUE)
```

## What the assistant chooses

**Cohort and scope.** The cohort may be SCI, a custom ICD list, Sepsis-3 or
the whole source. `import_scope` then decides how much
context is kept: `icu` keeps the selected stays, `hospital` keeps the admission
and its sibling ICU stays, `patient` keeps every encounter of the selected
patients. SCI and custom ICD cohorts select admissions on MIMIC, so ICU scope is
not offered there; sepsis selects ICU stays and accepts all three. The default
for Sepsis-3 is `hospital`, matching `build_sepsis_cohort()`.

**Analysis level and merge.** These are independent. The analysis level decides
how the harmonized tables are grained; the merge decides whether the pooled
schema is written at admission or ICU grain. An ICU merge builds the ICU
timeline tables even when the analysis level is admission.

**Engine.** A DuckDB plan loads the raw data, builds the generic cohort
information and runs the concept layers that DuckDB supports, then stops. To
continue in PostgreSQL, generate a PostgreSQL plan and start at `run_db()`,
reusing the cohort Parquet.

**Configuration.** The assistant can write missing configuration files, use an
existing project, or skip configuration. It never overwrites an existing
`ehr_config.json` or credentials file. In manual mode the script writes a
comment between creating and loading the configuration, but it does not pause
execution; run the generated blocks one at a time. Paths are recorded as absolute paths, and
the nearest existing ancestor must be writable.

## Sepsis variants in the assistant

| `sepsis_bundle` | Packaged selection |
|:--|:--|
| `sepsis3` | Every qualifying ICU stay |
| `first_sepsis3` | First-ever ICU stay, if it qualifies |
| `sepsis3_admission` | Suspicion within ±1 day of ICU admission |
| `first_sepsis3_admission` | Both restrictions |

The assistant only assembles calls; the definitions themselves are the packaged
SQL described in `vignette("sepsis-cohort-definitions")`.

## Non-interactive use

With `interactive = FALSE` the assistant returns the plan immediately. Values
you leave out take the documented defaults (MIMIC-IV demo, SCI cohort, patient
scope), so a non-interactive call is a reproducible way to generate a standard
script. An invalid explicit combination fails during planning instead of being
silently widened.

